  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 946 PM MST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nUpper ridge continues to build over the Great Basin and Desert \nSouthwest, ahead of a deepening Pacific trough still off the west \ncoast of the US. Meanwhile the sub-tropical jet remains active with \ndeep layered sub-tropical moisture, but feature will remain well \nsouth of our area. Thus the upper ridge is the dominating playa over \ntonight into early Friday, with mostly clear skies and seasonable \ntemperatures. \n\nFriday and Saturday...Pacific trough finally moves onshore as it \ncontinues to dig as very strong polar jet dives down the back side \nof the trough. As the Gulf of Alaska upper low weakens, a closed off \nlow will form at the bottom of the trough and swing across the \nnorthern Baja Friday afternoon. This low will contain a decent \namount of moisture and all three of GFS/ECMWF/RRFS showing low able \nto tap a bit of the sub-tropical moisture over Mexico. Good chance \nfor showers to develop and make it as far east as the RG Valley by \nlate Friday afternoon. GFS still shows good cold air aloft (about 5C \ndeg cooling at 500mb), which should lead to decent instability with \nvery modest CAPES. Because of this, an isolated thunderstorm will be \npossible with the showers. With the upper low tracking across \nnorthern Mexico or far southern New Mexico Friday night along with \nthe surface cold front, showers will spread east over the rest of \nthe area. Expect broad area of .25-.50 of rainfall across the area, \nwith a few spots maybe hitting 1.0 inch with a thunderstorm. \nMost/all showers should be done in the east by mid day Saturday as \nthe low quickly moves into central Texas. Snow levels will remain \nhigh, but an inch or so of snow is possible above 8500 ft. Rapidly \nmoving upper low associated with the polar jet moves in right behind \nthe exiting low but will track across Colorado Saturday night. Rain \nchances should remain north of the CWA, but could see some breezy \nwinds Saturday night.\n\nSunday and Monday...short wave ridge sweeps in Sunday behind the \nexiting lows. Expect mostly clear skies again, but temperatures will \nbe cool, a bit below normal, behind the cold front.\n\nTuesday and Wednesday...next Pacific system moves in with the low \ntracking across SoCal and Arizona. Both GFS/ECMWF similar with the \ntrack but ECMWF about 6-10 hours faster than GFS. Rain chances \nincrease as early as Tuesday mid day and are likely done by mid day \nWednesday. Temperatures will remain below normal.",
